# Persona 2: Tsumi (SLPS-02100): the game's own character table.
# 16-bit little-endian glyph numbers, keys in memory byte order. Number n is the n-th glyph of the 12x12 font the
# game keeps in memory, so the table follows the font: 0x0000-0x007F marks, numerals, the first kana and ASCII;
# from 0x0080 kana, kanji and marks in the game's own order.
# Read from the font by eye, then proofread against the disc: every message was decoded and each kanji checked
# in the words around it. A kanji is listed only when it is confirmed, either by a word list (at least three
# dictionary words, a third of its uses or more) or by reading its uses one by one beside the enlarged glyph.
# Persona 2: Batsu (SLPS-02785) has the same glyphs up to 0x0A0E and lists this table first: codes its script
# confirmed the same way are listed here too, also when this game's script never uses them.
# 2181 kanji, 146 kana and 23 marks from 0x0080 are listed. Left out on purpose, so they read as U+FFFD:
# 33 codes from 0x0080 this game's script uses that stayed in doubt or are blank glyphs (125 of the 582651
# uses on the disc), 0x005E (a blank glyph), and the codes neither game's messages confirm.
# The control words (0x11xx, and 0x12xx with one argument word) are in the entry, but for three that only leave
# a gap in the line (0x111F, 0x1120, 0x1121: the indent every line of a message starts with, and the gap between
# two words): they are listed last, as a space. The layout's cleanup takes the indent off again.
